\kerning0\cf0\ftnbj\fet2\ftnstart1\ftnnar\aftnnar\ftnstart1\aftnstart1\aenddoc\revprop3{\info\uc1}\deftab720\viewkind1\paperw12240\paperh15840\margl1440\margr1440\widowctrl
\sectd\sbknone\colsx360\pgncont\ltrsect
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s29\itap0{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab      April 8, 2007}{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s29\itap0{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s29\itap0{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch  }{\s29\f0\fs28\ul\lang1033{\*\listtag0}Thoughts and suggestions on the assembly and calibration of the Ion_Pump_Controller _6(A)}{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s29\itap0{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch 1) load and solder all of the axial leaded components except for the 300 M Ohm resistor(resistors and diodes) first. Create a solder loop in the10K isolation resistor of the current monitor circuit. This is not necessary in the _6A version of the circuit board.}{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s29\itap0{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch 2) Align the color code(s) left-to-right and top-to-bottom for easier reading.}{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s29\itap0{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch 3) Dip sockets are optional. IC replacement is much easier if it becomes necessary.}{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s29\itap0{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch 4) load non polarized capacitors.}{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s29\itap0{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch 5) load one polarity of the polarized capacitors, solder them in place, and then load the other polarity.}{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s29\itap0{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch 6) install and solder the TO-92 packaged devices. Note that the 317L in the _6 version of the circuit boards has two leads that must be transposed. This is corrected in the _6A version of the circuit boards.}{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s29\itap0{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch 7) check the pin alignment of the E-50 high voltage converter and straighten as necessary. Load the Mil-Max sockets onto the E-50 pins and insert the assembly into the PCB. Solder in place. Remove the E_50.}{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s29\itap0{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch 8) clean the PCB}{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s29\itap0{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch 9) Install the IRF 9250 P channel MOS FET with the proper electrical isolation hardware and thermally conductive grease.}{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s29\itap0{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch 10) install the LEDs, reset switch, phono-jack, trim pots, and the fuse holder.}{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s29\itap0{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch 11) Install the 300M Ohm resistor with a small air gap between the resistor and the PCB. This is to prevent a high voltage breakdown from the body of the resistor to the ground plane.}{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s29\itap0{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch 12) carefully clean the new solder joints. Avoid contaminating the 300M Ohm resistor.}{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s29\itap0{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch 13) place the E-50 back into the Mil_Max sockets and tie it securely to the PCB.}{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s29\itap0{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch 14) If you have assembled the PCB with sockets, load all of the DIP ICs.}{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s29\itap0{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch 15) Install a 250mA slow blow fuse.}{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s29\itap0{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch 16) Add a +12 and ground line to the PCB.}{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s29\itap0{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch 17) Add four stand-offs on the corners of the PCB. }{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s29\itap0{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch 18) Pre-adjust a DC power supply to  a nominal +12V}{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s29\itap0{\s29\f0\fs28\ul\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch NOTE THAT THE PCB BECOMES DANGEROUS WITH POWER APPLIED!}{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\tab }{\s29\f0\fs28\ul\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s29\itap0{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch 19) Place the circuit on a clean non-Ohmic work surface.}{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s29\itap0{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch 20) Turn the power on while monitoring the DC supply's output current. It should start at a low value and smoothly rise to ~ 100mA (could be a bit more or less as this is based the }{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}performance of }{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}only 2 units)}{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s29\itap0{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch 21) With a 10G Ohm high voltage probe adjust the +HV to a nominal +3500V and then shut of the external power}{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s29\itap0{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch 22) Install the functioning but not yet calibrated circuit into the bottom half of the chassis.}{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s29\itap0{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch 23) Connect the HV connector.}{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s29\itap0{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch 24) Repower the circuit and add the 40M Ohm load to the HV connector. Check the current monitor for the proper increase(5V= 100uA). }{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s29\itap0{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch 25) remove the 40M Ohm load and reduce the +3500V to ~+3400V}{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s29\itap0{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch 26) add the 30M Ohm load and slowly increase the HV back to +3500V. If the overcurrent circuit trips remove the 30M Ohms and adjust the trip point higher. 27)Iterate #25 and #26 until the circuit barely trips at 3500V and the30M Ohm load connected.}{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s29\itap0{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch 28) Check the functionality of the 3 LEDs. Remove the fuse to simulate the blown fuse, and cause a High Voltage fault with the 30M Ohm resistive load.}{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s29\itap0{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch 29) Check the functionality of the reset switch. If the switch is pressed with the 30M Ohm load still connected the circuit will try to start again and then return to the fault mode (correct function).}{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s29\itap0{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch 31) Finish the production wiring and then check the external monitor(s) and the external reset ( +5V and a few us wide pulse) }{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s29\itap0{\s29\f0\fs28\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}}